hey guys Greg Knuckles here with string  theory calm back with the ninth  installment of our daily Q&amp;A series and  the question today is a pretty common  one what are the best sources of  information for athletes or coaches to  build their knowledge base let me just  start by saying probably not blogs  probably not YouTube channels that  includes my website and that includes  this YouTube channel  once you've already dug into the  resources I'm going to suggest reading  stuff online watching YouTube videos as  long as you choose that information  judiciously that can be a good way to to  deepen your understanding maybe learn  you know a few more tips a little bit  more nuance that perhaps you weren't  aware of before but the things I'm going  to suggest in this video are I think  going to be better resources for most  people okay so first things first I  think the best thing the best place to  start is going to be with textbooks I  know that's not a particularly popular  solution most people don't like just  sitting down and reading a textbook but  when it comes to really gaining both a  large breadth and depth of knowledge you  really can't do better than a textbook I  think the biggest benefit you get out of  a textbook is just it really helps you  hone your [ __ ] detector i I see a  lot of questions comments feedback on  videos I make articles I write from  people asking about something that  probably wouldn't be a question at all  if they just understood really basic  physiology or really basic anatomy or  really basic biomechanics and it's one  of those things like textbooks they  aren't fun  they aren't fun to read but if you read  them and understand them and it sticks  with you it's going to make everything  else you  from then on out it's going to make that  much much more beneficial because you'll  have a lot easier time identifying good  information and identifying bad  information know what to stick with know  what to avoid and also you'll have more  context for the information that you do  learn so it'll probably stick with you  better and be more beneficial for you so  with that in mind the textbooks that I  would recommend every lift or get is one  just a basic anatomy and physiology  textbook I have the 1 by beta land Earl  I think it's very good X in exercise  physiology textbook I have will more  Kinney and cos well I think it's very  good for more on strength training  specifically the science and practice of  strength training bodies at sea or ski  and Kramer is exceptional and then round  that out with really any good  introductory biomechanics textbook and a  human nutrition or sports nutrition  textbook most of them are going to have  the vast majority of the same  information and I'm not particularly  partial to any of them so that is where  you should start the next thing is going  to be talking to people who have a solid  education background who have also  accomplished the things you want to  accomplish so you know talking to other  athletes and coaches who you know have  worked with athletes you want to work  with or who have accomplished something  in their sport that you want to  accomplish so really a deep  understanding that's going to be the  marriage of both you know just facts  book learning with experience that's  that's where true deep understanding  comes from and so at first you can't you  can't hack experience you can't  meaningfully speed up that timeline you  just have to put in the work and put in  the years and so with textbooks that is  going to give you that knowledge base  and you can acquire that pretty quickly  I mean textbook 600 pages put in 20 or  30 pages a night  you know you can read the textbook in a  month 20 days again it's not going to be  fun to read but you can pick up a lot of  information very quickly that way that's  that's going to be the fastest part of  the education process and then talking  to people who have been at it for a long  time and have accomplished what you want  to accomplish that's not as good as your  own experience but you're also going to  be able to glean lessons from their  experience to you know give you things  to look out for in in your own  experience in your own training in your  own coaching and then after that just  time I've been I've been lifting now for  twelve years and I've been coaching for  seven eight and I mean still to this day  like I I will tend to defer to lifters  who you know may have not lifted as much  as I have but if they've been in the  game for twenty thirty years they'll  probably have insights about the  training process that I don't have and  same thing with coaches coaches who have  been doing doing it for a long time and  have really been making the efforts to  continually improve you know I I think  I'm pretty good for for the amount of  time and experience that I have but you  know I I can't put myself in a time  machine and just speed up twenty thirty  years of experience of learning so  number one teach yourself in textbooks  right away learn those as quickly as you  can and refer back to them constantly  make sure that information stays in mind  next thing talk to people who have that  life experience and then can help kind  of guide you show you the way and then  after that it's it's just a matter of  training coaching for a long long time  with a with a conscious effort for  getting better that's those are the  three in my opinion those are the three  best sources of education for most  people and then you know again once  you've  once you've done the first and second  steps as you're putting in the reps as  you're putting in the time reading blogs  watching YouTube channels that stuff is  good but it's it's not going to be as  meaningful it's not going to give you  the same depth and breadth of  understanding as talking to people who  have walked the walk and as you know  building that knowledge base from  reading textbooks so yeah that's that's  my basic answer to that question and I  know some people like I on some level I  know that that's not particularly  helpful advice because I do know a lot  of people just simply won't read  textbooks I hey I've been recommending  this for a long time and I think maybe a  half dozen people have actually gone out  and picked up the textbooks I've  recommended and actually read them so if  if you really really don't think you're  going to go that route some other good  resources I would recommend my own here  the art and science of lifting by myself  and Oh Mart you saw the muscle and  strength of pyramids by Eric Helms are  also very very good and scientific  principles of strength training and the  Renaissance five by Mike written by Mike  dis Ratelle are also very very good so  if you if you are really really against  reading textbooks those would also be  some good suggestions but I would not  recommend any of those resources my own  included over starting out over starting  out by reading textbooks so again just  to recap read textbooks talk to people  who who have been walking the wall for a  long time to try to learn from their  experiences and then just spend the time  training coaching gaining that  experience which is invaluable but you  can't you can't rush it just takes time  so that is the video if you liked it  please like and subscribe and if you  have questions ask them in the comments  below and if yours is my favorite I will  pick an answer it in that next  video so until then hope your training  is good hope you have a great day and I  will see you in the next video
 

